If there is an opioid-policy-setter then it is recommended that the opioid-policy-setter does not set a rigid-dose-standard with a CDC-clinical-practice-guideline.
If there is an opioid-policy-setter then it is recommended that the opioid-policy-setter does not set a rigid-duration-standard with a CDC-clinical-practice-guideline.
If there is an opioid-policy-setter then it is recommended that the opioid-policy-setter does not set a dose-or-duration-performance-incentive with a CDC-clinical-practice-guideline.
Every opioid-policy-setter should ensure a cautionary-threshold-policy that does not cause a rapid-opioid-taper.
Every opioid-policy-setter should ensure a cautionary-threshold-policy that does not cause an abrupt-opioid-discontinuation.
If there is an opioid-policy-setter then it is recommended that the opioid-policy-setter does not penalize a clinician for a new-chronic-pain-prescribed-opioid-patient-acceptance.
If there is an opioid-policy-setter then it is recommended that the opioid-policy-setter does not penalize a clinician that does not rapidly taper a long-term-opioid-medication for a patient.
A new-chronic-pain-prescribed-opioid-patient-acceptance-example is a high-dosage-patient-acceptance-example.
Every payer is an opioid-policy-setter.
Every health-system is an opioid-policy-setter.
Every state-medical-board is an opioid-policy-setter.
